    <!-- OVERVIEW SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="overview" class="doc-h2">1. Overview</h2>
            <p class="doc-body">This plan defines how we use AI in TWO layers and the migration path between them, and records a real notification regression that must be fixed independently.</p>
            <ul class="doc-body">
                <li><strong>In-app agents</strong> (user-facing, live in the AI2 / AI-editor surface, NOT Hermes): HelpDesk, Beekeeping, Ency (herb research). Already "in the works." Migration target is the AI editor, not Hermes.</li>
                <li><strong>Operational agents</strong> (run outside the app via Hermes/cron, later migrate into the AI editor): the <strong>Email agent</strong> (fills the missing input) and the <strong>Secretary agent</strong> (reads the DB, judges what is critical, and <strong>calls you</strong> via your registered VoIP number — text/voice/in-browser). The Secretary is the reference example of an agent that <strong>orchestrates other agents and uses more than one model</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="doc-body"><strong>Hermes stays the coding agent.</strong> The AI editor is the in-app AI host. Standalone operational agents migrate INTO the editor as it matures; they are not built on Hermes long-term.</p>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- REGRESSION SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="regression" class="doc-h2">2. Notification regression (real defect — fix separately)</h2>
            <p class="doc-body">Historically the app notified via <strong>in-app + system + email</strong>. Today <strong>only email still works</strong>; the in-app and system notification paths are broken/regressed. This is a genuine defect, independent of the agent work, and is logged as a blocking todo (NOT folded into Phase 1). Restore both paths before relying on any agent notification.</p>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- TWO CLASSES SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="twoclasses" class="doc-h2">3. Two classes of agent (do not conflate)</h2>
            <ul class="doc-body">
                <li><strong>(a) Branch-context files</strong> (<code>.hermes.md</code>) — already built. These are NOT agents; they are static context that keeps a general coding agent on-track inside a worktree. They must contain ONLY the branch's own domain + code-ownership + the global compliance rules (by pointer). The full branch map lives in the coordinator (main <code>.hermes.md</code> + this plan's sibling <code>HermesBranchContextPlan</code>).</li>
                <li><strong>(b) Specialized agents</strong> — purpose-built for RECURRING, BOUNDED, AUTONOMOUS work. They earn their keep only when the task repeats and is well-scoped. The Secretary/Email agents qualify; a 3d/inventory <em>coding</em> agent does NOT yet (still human-supervised worktree flow — a SKILL is the lighter right tool there).</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- OPERATIONAL AGENTS SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="operational" class="doc-h2">4. Operational agents (outside app; migrate to AI editor later)</h2>

            <h3 id="email-agent" class="doc-h3">4.1 Email agent — the missing input</h3>
            <p class="doc-body">The production-health / serious-error &#8594; DB &#8594; DailyPlan pipeline ALREADY exists and is the signal source. The gap is <strong>email</strong>. The Email agent scans/summarizes mail (reuse himalaya/IMAP — not a new channel) and feeds the existing DB/DailyPlan flow. It does not rebuild monitoring; it adds the missing input.</p>

            <h3 id="secretary-agent" class="doc-h3">4.2 Secretary agent — orchestrator + VoIP call</h3>
            <p class="doc-body">The Secretary <strong>evaluates the DB</strong> (todos, serious errors, calendar) for critical items and <strong>calls you</strong> when something needs your attention. It is the multi-agent example you asked for:</p>
            <ul class="doc-body">
                <li><strong>Orchestrator</strong> (cheap local model — Ollama): decides what to check, fans out.</li>
                <li><strong>Sub-agent A</strong> (cheap): DB evaluation/triage of todos + errors + calendar.</li>
                <li><strong>Sub-agent B</strong> (stronger — OpenRouter or Grok): when something is critical, DRAFT the notification and summarize for the user.</li>
                <li><strong>Reach you via VoIP</strong>: a NEW user-VoIP-number config page (successor to the old Perl4 "call a page" feature) lets the Secretary call you by <strong>text, voice, or in-browser</strong>. You can interact back via text / voice / browser. Models available today in the AI chat system: Ollama (local/cheap), OpenRouter, Grok.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="doc-body"><strong>Reuse, do not duplicate:</strong> the Secretary consumes the existing error/todo signal; it does not re-implement health checks. Gateway/VoIP delivery only — never ad-hoc SMTP.</p>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- IN-APP AGENTS SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="inapp" class="doc-h2">5. In-app agents (HelpDesk / Beekeeping / Ency) — BLOCKED</h2>
            <p class="doc-body">These cannot be built until their <strong>blocking todos exist</strong> and are listed in DailyPlan. Phase 2 enumerates those blockers and adds them to DailyPlan first; only then do we formalize each agent's boundary. <strong>Beekeeping (BMaster)</strong> is the cleanest isolated domain and the first candidate. These currently live outside Hermes and will migrate into the AI editor.</p>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECRETARY SUB-PROJECT SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="secretary" class="doc-h2">6. Secretary sub-project (where it lives)</h2>
            <p class="doc-body">The Secretary agent is tracked as a <strong>sub-project of #267 (AGENTS — Autonomous Agents, job-specific)</strong>, NOT under the Planning project. (Parent #267 already has the agent taxonomy: 268 Agent Framework Core, 269 Built-in Job Agents, 270 Agent Ops &amp; Safety.) The exact Secretary sub-project ID must be created in the app (the <code>/api/projects</code> write did not persist in this session — create it via the project UI, then stamp its ID here). Until then, reference it as "<strong>SECRETARY-AGENT</strong>, child of #267."</p>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- PRINCIPLES SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="principles" class="doc-h2">7. Principles (avoid agent sprawl)</h2>
            <ul class="doc-body">
                <li><strong>Registry single source</strong> — one place defines agents (proposed: <code>root/config/agents.json</code> or this Documentation plan). No drifting copies.</li>
                <li><strong>Reuse before create</strong> — agents reuse existing pipelines (error-audit, cron, gateway, VoIP); never reinvent.</li>
                <li><strong>Observability</strong> — every agent action goes through <code>Logging-&gt;log_with_details</code>. No silent agents.</li>
                <li><strong>Deliver via gateway / VoIP</strong> — never direct SMTP hacks.</li>
                <li><strong>Skill vs agent</strong> — a one-off domain task = SKILL (loadable by any agent); a recurring autonomous task = AGENT. Keeps the count down.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- PHASING SECTION -->
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-100">
            <h2 id="phasing" class="doc-h2">8. Phasing</h2>
            <ul class="doc-body">
                <li><strong>Phase 0</strong> — Author this plan + registry shape; log the notification-regression blocking todo; create the SECRETARY-AGENT sub-project under #267.</li>
                <li><strong>Phase 1</strong> — Email agent (fill the gap) + Secretary agent (DB-eval &#8594; VoIP call, text/voice/browser) + the user-VoIP-number config page. Reuses existing error/todo signal.</li>
                <li><strong>Phase 2</strong> — Enumerate in-app trio (HelpDesk/Beekeeping/Ency) blockers; add to DailyPlan; formalize each agent's boundary. Beekeeping first.</li>
                <li><strong>Phase 3</strong> — Migrate operational agents into the AI editor when it can host them; retire the standalone copy.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>
        </div>
    </div>

